Here is a simple approach to recording audio using SwiftUI. This example uses AVFoundation to record audio and SwiftUI to create the user interface.

First, create a new SwiftUI project in Xcode. In the ContentView.swift file, import AVFoundation and add the following code:

```swift
import SwiftUI
import AVFoundation

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var isRecording = false
    @State private var recorder: AVAudioRecorder?
    @State private var audioURL: URL?

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            if isRecording {
                Text("Recording...")
                    .foregroundColor(.red)
            } else {
                Text("Tap to start recording")
                    .foregroundColor(.green)
            }

            Button(action: {
                if isRecording {
                    stopRecording()
                } else {
                    startRecording()
                }
            }) {
                Text(isRecording ? "Stop" : "Start")
            }
        }
        .padding()
        .onAppear {
            startRecording()
        }
    }

    private func startRecording() {
        let audioSession = AVAudioSession.sharedInstance()
        do {
            try audioSession.setCategory(.playAndRecord, mode: .default)
            try audioSession.setActive(true)

            let settings = [
                AVFormatIDKey: Int(kAudioFormatMPEG4AAC),
                AVSampleRateKey: 12000,
                AVNumberOfChannelsKey: 1,
                AVEncoderAudioQualityKey: AVAudioQuality.high.rawValue
            ]

            let documentDirectory = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
            let audioFilename = documentDirectory.appendingPathComponent("recording.m4a")
            audioURL = audioFilename

            recorder = try AVAudioRecorder(url: audioFilename, settings: settings)
            recorder?.prepareToRecord()
            recorder?.record()

            isRecording = true
        } catch {
            print("Error starting recording: \(error.localizedDescription)")
        }
    }

    private func stopRecording() {
        recorder?.stop()
        isRecording = false
    }
}
```

This code creates a simple SwiftUI view with a button that starts and stops recording. When the recording starts, it creates an AVAudioRecorder instance and sets up the audio session. When the recording stops, it saves the recorded audio to a file in the app's document directory.

Note that this is a very simple example and does not include any error handling or audio playback. For a more complete solution, you may want to consider using a library like AVFoundation or a third-party framework like SwiftAudio.

