```cpp
// Define the pins for the motor controller
const int motor1A = 9; // Pin for motor 1, channel A
const int motor1B = 10; // Pin for motor 1, channel B
const int motor2A = 11; // Pin for motor 2, channel A
const int motor2B = 12; // Pin for motor 2, channel B

void setup() {
  // Set the pins as outputs
  pinMode(motor1A, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(motor1B, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(motor2A, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(motor2B, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  // Move motor 1 forward
  digitalWrite(motor1A, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(motor1B, LOW);

  // Move motor 2 backward
  digitalWrite(motor2A, LOW);
  digitalWrite(motor2B, HIGH);

  delay(1000); // Wait for 1 second

  // Stop both motors
  digitalWrite(motor1A, LOW);
  digitalWrite(motor1B, LOW);
  digitalWrite(motor2A, LOW);
  digitalWrite(motor2B, LOW);

  delay(1000); // Wait for 1 second
}
```

In this example, we define the pins for the motor controller and set them as outputs in the `setup()` function. In the `loop()` function, we first move motor 1 forward and motor 2 backward for 1 second, then stop both motors for 1 second. This process repeats indefinitely.

Please note that you should adjust the pin numbers and the direction of the motors according to your specific setup. Also, make sure to connect the motors and the power supply correctly to the motor driver board.

